+At Caltech, MRG-GCN achieves an overall MAE of 0.03890 kWh/15 min, 16.1% below a near-capacity-matched TCN, while full AGCRN has a lower MAE. The advantage is concentrated on zero-target keys; nonzero-target and event errors do not improve over TCN. Node-specific gates reduce overall MAE by 2.69% at Caltech and 2.71% at JPL relative to equal-parameter shared gates; one shared-gate seed at each site reaches the training limit, so this result is conditional on the tested budget. Post-hoc equal-parameter retraining at JPL supports conditional overall-MAE gains from load-coupling and profile-similarity graphs relative to self-loop replacements: 1.82% and 1.52%, with multiplicity-adjusted intervals above zero. The behavioral co-occurrence result remains uncertain. An exploratory stratification finds a nonzero-group increment for the load relation relative to its self-loop replacement, but this component contrast does not mean that the complete MRG-GCN beats TCN on nonzero keys; it does not do so at Caltech, JPL, or under the completion-truncated target. The latter target preserves the global MAE ranking. Both supplements reuse previously inspected test periods.
 
 MDP distinguishes functional dependence from incremental accuracy. Frozen β replacements reveal joint-configuration sensitivity; reparameterization and equal-weight retraining do not establish an independent accuracy benefit from β. The tested distillation does not close the teacher–student gap, and the four-cell decomposition distinguishes rare difficult keys from the larger cumulative contribution of common keys. Interval evaluation further shows that JPL coverage near 90% overall coexists with inadequate event coverage, while rolling calibration at Caltech requires substantially wider intervals. The thesis provides controlled incremental evidence and stratified diagnosis for within-station proxy-load forecasting. Metered-power validity, reporting delays, and field benefits require synchronized measurements and operational evaluation.
 
